================================================================================
THE ERROR, AND IT IS THE ONE CHAPTER9-PAINPOINTS ALREADY NAMES
================================================================================

I predicted the Correspondence Theorem was BLOCKED, reasoning that it quantifies over
SUBGROUPS of G/N and a subgroup is a predicate with no sort of predicates to range
over. I wrote that into the survey README as "expect this to be the one theorem
stated-but-not-transliterated". Then I probed it. All of it proves — both directions,
surjectivity, and the injectivity step I most expected to fail.

CHAPTER9-PAINPOINTS states the rule I broke, in those words: "All three guesses came
from reasoning about what std HAS rather than what the language can EXPRESS, and all
three were settled in minutes by writing the declarations and running the checker.
Probe by writing the file, not by reading the library."

I quoted that file's rule back in the same survey where I made the error. The lesson
is not "remember the rule" — I did remember it — but that a prediction of
unreachability is worth nothing against twenty minutes of probing, so SKIP THE
PREDICTION and probe.

What was genuinely unreachable turned out to be narrower than the prediction AND
narrower than the previous chapter's: "S = H/N" is extensional agreement of two
predicates, which is what set equality MEANS when a subgroup is a predicate. Only the
"one-to-one correspondence between the two SETS" phrasing is out of reach, and that is
the SAME =~ limit §9.1 settled. The chapter has ONE limit appearing in four places,
not four limits.

================================================================================
THE LIBRARY GATE CAUGHT THREE DESIGN PROBLEMS, NOT THREE FORMALITIES
================================================================================

`check --library std` fails on an axiom no theorem rests on. Three times this chapter
it was reporting something real:

1. homomorphism.b4m, `opGAssoc` unused. G's associativity declared, nothing resting on
   it — the file was treating G as a SET WITH A MAP rather than as a group, and
   `GIsAGroup` was declared and never cited. Fixed by proving
   kernel-trivial-iff-injective, which is what actually consumes G's group structure
   (cancellation in G). Without the gate that ships as a file whose source group is
   decorative.

2. factor.b4m, ALL FOUR of N's axioms unused. `clsCongruent` and `opQHom` between them
   already encode that N is a normal subgroup, so the lifted group laws never cite N's
   structure. The gate was saying N was decorative. Fixed by proving, FROM N's axioms,
   the facts the posited ones require: the congruence is reflexive (needs the identity)
   and symmetric (needs inverse-closure), and the class operation is well-defined
   (needs NORMALITY — Judson's Thm 10.4, transferred through a model of subgroup.b4m).
   A consumer owes exactly those, so proving them shows the posits are satisfiable
   rather than assumed out of thin air.

3. correspondence.b4m, three unused (H's inverse-closure, H's containment of N, H2's
   identity). Each became a real theorem: H/N's inverse-closure half, the injectivity
   proof, H2/N's identity.

The pattern: an unused axiom in a theory file means the theory is assuming structure
it does not use, which is nearly always a missing theorem rather than a spurious
axiom.

================================================================================
MECHANICS WORTH KEEPING
================================================================================

AN UNCITED `fact` ALIAS IS NEVER RESOLVED. I first wrote aata/10.1's re-exports as
`fact X = ns.y` aliases, then tested by pointing one at `group_factor.noSuchThing` —
the file still reported OK. References are demand-driven, so an alias nothing cites is
never looked up. Not a soundness hole (citing it catches it immediately, same as a
`where <undeclared>` guard) but an alias-only re-export PROVES NOTHING. Use a THEOREM
with a one-line `[using import(I) thm]` proof: that forces the target to exist AND its
statement to match. Every re-export in aata/11.1 and 11.2 is written that way.

`assoc` CANNOT INSERT A CANCELLING PAIR. Both of Judson's Second-Isomorphism
regroupings —
    (h1 n1)(h2 n2) = h1 h2 (h2^-1 n1 h2) n2
    (hn)^-1        = h^-1 (h n^-1 h^-1)
— insert h2 h2^-1 / h^-1 h, so the two sides are NOT equal by associativity alone.
`assoc` right-nests both sides and compares; it cannot invent the pair. Each needs the
cancellation written out (flatten, regroup, cancel, absorb the identity).

A `define` ALIAS NEEDS ITS PARAMETER. `define inImage = homomorphism.inImage` fails
with "expects 0 arguments, got 1"; write `define inImage(x) = homomorphism.inImage(x)`.

`group.inverseIdentity` LIVES IN std/group/sequence.b4m, not std/group.b4m, so it is
NOT on a model's transfer path. invH(EH) = EH is three lines from inverse-uniqueness.
Same for G's right-inverse law in correspondence.b4m — factor.b4m does not re-export
it, so a local model of std/group.b4m at G's symbols is the route.

A TRANSFERRED AXIOM MAY ARRIVE IN THE OPPOSITE ORIENTATION. `opGAssoc` transferred
through a model gave `op(op(a,b),c) = op(a,op(b,c))` reversed from the source axiom, so
regrouping needed a `symmetry`. Check the orientation rather than assuming it.

NORMALITY ORIENTATION. std states it as `inN(n) -> inN(g n g^-1)`. Judson writes
h^-1 n h, which is the axiom at g = h^-1, giving h^-1 n (h^-1)^-1 — so an
`invInvolution` rewrite is needed to reach his form. The inverse half of the HN proof
needs no flip: h n^-1 h^-1 is already the axiom's shape at g = h.

